our series rebuilding america showcases  people and communities that are finding  creative wills to build a better world  one project at a time this morning we  head to florida where the manatee  population is facing a crisis at least  600 have died so far this year many from  starvation poor water quality is  destroying the sea grass they rely on  for food arlene kiano traveled to  crystal river florida also known as the  manatee capital of the world to see one  community's innovative solution  when paul cross navigates crystal river  and kings bay florida he can't help but  remember when algae choked these waters  and suffocated native sea grass the  primary food source for florida manatees  we wouldn't even do manatee tours in the  summer months there wasn't as many  manatees that stayed because we didn't  have the food source and the visibility  when we did find those manatees was very  poor  as director of operations for plantation  adventure center a local dive shop cross  watched the river decline years ago  storms and septic tank pollution had  taken a toll  lush green beds of sea grass had been  overrun by thick mats of invasive algae  and with no food the manatees had nearly  disappeared  these tropical marine mammals can weigh  up to three thousand pounds and they  flock here in winter months to graze in  the warmth of the 72-degree waters when  their numbers dwindled along with other  marine life lisa moore realized the  river was dying personally i thought how  did i let this happen  how did i not know that this was going  on  and i've lived here all this time i  wasn't paying attention and so i kind of  felt like i needed to try to do  something the dying river compelled  moore a lifelong resident to create an  organization called save crystal river  in 2015 the group hired an aquatic  restoration company with an untested  idea  marine biologist ryan brushwood works  for that company sea and shoreline so  this is the bad guy the story uh this is  lingbia as you can see it's some gross  stuff it's like the stuff at the bottom  of the tub when the drain clogs right it  feels like the stuff that that clogs  your drains and here it was clogging  springs  and it was out competing  the grass just because of the sheer  quantity of it and over time as this  settled on the bottom of the seafloor  and started to cane it created a  sediment that was not suitable for the  plants to grow in  their plan was to send divers to the  bottom and use giant vacuums like these  to hoover up decades of algae and muck  they then plant seagrass back into the  bay by hand a painstaking expensive  process  armed with research from both the  company and independent scientists save  crystal river took its plan door to door  and then to state legislators in  tallahassee  eventually the group convinced lawmakers  to greenlight a 1.6 million dollar state  funded pilot project to restore about  three acres people around the community  a lot of them didn't believe that it  would work like i was the biggest  skeptic right when they started planting  grass and i'm like i've seen the  manatees in action i've seen them chew  roots from the trees like they're going  to devour all of this stuff  but six years later the beds of seagrass  and the restored areas are coming back  as are the manatees along with fish and  other marine life  as we saw on a recent swim with john  spann of plantation adventure center you  know five years ago you look down you  wouldn't see the bottom you couldn't  even see it in here and now you can swim  this hulk and out as you saw and you  could just see the grass see the bottom  and that's just that grass filtering  that water holding the sediment down  doing those good things that it's meant  to do residents here are also moving  from septic to sewer systems reducing  runoff that feeds harmful algae is it  starting to look like the river you  remember from your childhood yeah yeah  you go and you look at the springs  and you can see  all the way down in the springs again  you can clearly see the fish it's back  and it's just gonna get better i imagine  that must make you pretty happy it does  my children my grandchildren  all the other people that come here  visitors from around the world they need  to know what this special place is  the pilot program started with three  acres but they've since restored about  60 and they're hoping to restore more  than 90 acres by this time next year now  this is not cheap all that work will  cost about 40 million dollars but the  results are clear and now other  communities in florida with similar  problems are looking at this as a  potential solution  and manatees are so important to the  ecosystem yeah that's what people may  not realize that they keep it free of of  invasive species and overgrowth of  vegetation um and serve as a source of  fertilizer so that's why it's important  to save them yeah when we were setting  up the story earlier we were talking  about how humans have an impact on  things like this you say i was surprised  surprised here we are messing something  up again but we have an opportunity to  change it people have come together and  that's what i think is so extraordinary  about this story is that people didn't  realize at first what was happening  because they couldn't necessarily see  once they learned about it they figured  out how do we solve it what do we do we  got to go door-to-door we have to do the  hard work of convincing people convince  those neighbors convince the state  legislature now you have we need to fix  these you look very comfortable in the  water there miss kiana i love the water  fish i love those manatees elaine's next  chapter  of 20 years i'll gladly be your aquatic  correspondent all right why did course  go i like it we don't have one of those  yet elaine keano
 

